# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 have "self_cause → prior_self" and "¬prior_self"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 has "□(self_cause → prior_self)" and "¬◇prior_self"; they differ when only "necessarily, (if a thing is the efficient cause of itself, then a thing is prior to itself)" and "possibly, a thing is prior to itself" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "necessarily, (if a thing is the efficient cause of itself, then a thing is prior to itself)" and "possibly, a thing is prior to itself" are true and "a thing is the efficient cause of itself" and "a thing is prior to itself" are false. Is S3, "There is no case known (neither is it, indeed, possible) in which a thing is found to be the efficient cause of itself; for so it would be prior to itself, which is impossible.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 has "chain"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 has "chain" and "chain → ¬infinite"; they differ when only "the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause" and "in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 has "order → chain"
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "in the world of sense there is an order of efficient causes", "the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause" and "in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity" are true. Is S4, "Now in efficient causes it is not possible to go on to infinity, because in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause, whether the intermediate cause be several, or only one.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 have "chain → (¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ (¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 has "chain ∧ ¬first → ¬intermediate ∧ ¬ultimate"; they differ when only "the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause", "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause", "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect" are true and "there is an intermediate efficient cause" is false. Is S5, "Now to take away the cause is to take away the effect.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 has "infinite → ¬first" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 have "infinite → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; they differ when only "there is an intermediate efficient cause", "there is an ultimate effect" and "in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there is an intermediate efficient cause", "there is an ultimate effect" and "in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity" are true and "there is a first efficient cause" is false. Is S7, "But if in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity, there will be no first efficient cause, neither will there be an ultimate effect, nor any intermediate efficient causes; all of which is plainly false.", true then?
